.vscode/**
src/**
webview-ui/**
vsix/**
# node_modules is ignored by default except for node-llama-cpp (and its own
# dependency tree, incl. the resolved @node-llama-cpp/<platform> optional
# package) — esbuild statically bundles every other dependency into
# dist/extension.js, so nothing else in node_modules is needed at runtime.
node_modules/**
!node_modules/node-llama-cpp/**
!node_modules/@node-llama-cpp/**
.gitignore
esbuild.mjs
tsconfig.json
tsconfig.test.json
jest.config.*
**/*.map
**/*.ts
**/*.tsx
*.vsix
*.tsbuildinfo
plan.md
TESTING.md
# NOTE: `esbuild`, `typescript`, and `jsonc-parser` are esbuild `external`
# (native binary / on-disk lib files / a UMD-bundling hazard — see esbuild.mjs's
# own comment for jsonc-parser's specific reason) and are NOT shipped: the
# `node_modules/**` rule above excludes them, and only node-llama-cpp is
# un-ignored. So deploy (esbuild), on-host type inference (typescript), and
# deploy's optional debug-source-map generation (jsonc-parser, reached only when
# workflowInsight.deployWithDebugInfo is on) still work in F5/dev only.
# Distribution of those needs a packaging step that vendors them (+ transitive
# deps) into `dist/node_modules/`, validated by installing a real .vsix. Backlog.
#
# `node-llama-cpp` IS shipped, which is why it is un-ignored above. Releases are
# built per platform (`vsce package --target <target>`, see
# vscode-extension-release.yml) on a runner matching each target, so npm resolves
# that platform's native binary and a getLlama() smoke test fails the build if it
# didn't. The local (on-device) model provider therefore works in a released
# .vsix, not just in dev.
#
# (An earlier version of this note said node-llama-cpp was excluded too, on the
# grounds that `vsce package` ran with `--no-dependencies`. That flag was dropped
# from the `package` scripts when per-target releases were introduced, so the note
# had become wrong in a way that also made AI-DISCLOSURE-REVIEW.md wrong.)
